## What this lane was

DERIVATION lane: the macaque gait controller derived from the admitted movement data
and authored as a contract — the document that makes the monkey WALK once the
free-root and joint-ladder packets land. Deliverables:

1. `docs/research/20260918_gait_controller_derivation.md` — the full derivation:
   gait cycle (duty 0.683 sampled vs paper 0.67, toe-off 68%, T = 0.71 s),
   joint-trajectory tables (21-node piecewise-linear phase tables, rad + deg, max
   reconstruction error 4.1 deg on MP), GRF profile (peak 106.5 N = 1.082 BW at 3%,
   loading rate 13.2 kN/s, stance impulse 34.48 N·s, closure 2·I_stance = BW·T_cycle
   at −1.3%), torque requirements (peaks hip 8.97 / knee 5.31 / ankle 5.92 / MP
   0.71 N·m; positive work per stride 5.25/2.56/3.27/0.50 J), the actuator-budget
   VERDICT (the qualified 2.0 J per-drive store does NOT power one stride — hip
   needs 3.25 J at arm scale; derived store floor = 1.5×W+_d per drive), the
   controller (contact-reset phase clock + tables + the qualified capped
   mass-normalized PD at f_s = 4.0 Hz, derived from a 0.95 amplitude-ratio criterion
   at the 1.41 Hz gait fundamental; the qualified 2.0 Hz servo is falsified for gait
   tracking), support-polygon stability with a capture-step reflex, falsifiers
   F-G1..F-G8, and the staged ladder A..G (frozen arm → 7-coord → free root →
   hindlimb lift → stand → walk → quadrupedal/substrates).
2. `tools/science_funnel/validation/gait_controller_20260918/derive_gait_numbers.py`
   + `derived_numbers.json` — the deterministic derivation script (stdlib-only xlsx
   reader; reads ONLY the pinned Oku MOESM2 xlsx and the pinned Janisch CSV) and its
   byte-stable machine output (sha256-identical across re-runs). Body anchor: Oku
